    "method_card": {
      "steps": [
        "Find the gradient: m = (y₂ − y₁) / (x₂ − x₁)",
        "Substitute one point into y = mx + c to find c",
        "Write the full equation y = mx + c",
        "For parallel lines: same gradient, different intercept",
        "For perpendicular lines: gradient is −1/m"
      ],
      "title": "How to Find the Equation of a Line",
      "content": "<p>Every straight line can be written as \\(y = mx + c\\) where \\(m\\) is the gradient and \\(c\\) is the y-intercept.</p><p>To find the equation from two points: (1) calculate the gradient, (2) substitute one point to find \\(c\\).</p><p><strong>Parallel lines</strong> have equal gradients. <strong>Perpendicular lines</strong> have gradients that multiply to \\(-1\\): if one gradient is \\(m\\), the other is \\(-\\frac{1}{m}\\).</p>",
      "example": "<p><strong>Find the equation of the line through (1, 4) and (3, 10).</strong></p><p>\\(m = \\frac{10-4}{3-1} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3\\)</p><p>Sub (1, 4): \\(4 = 3(1) + c \\Rightarrow c = 1\\)</p><p>Equation: \\(y = 3x + 1\\)</p>"
    },
